{
"content_id": "132938",
"reference_id": 108992,
"version": "1.0.4",
"title": "1 Thessalonians 2:1–16",
"media_type": "Text",
"index_reference": "52002001-52002016",
"language": "eng",
"review_level": "None",
"content": "<p>When Paul, Timothy and Silas preached to the Thessalonians, they had been sincere. They didn’t do it to be praised by anyone. They didn’t do it to gain control or power over anyone. They were gentle and humble like children. They were caring like mothers who love their children. They were like fathers who give their children hope and show them how to live.</p><p>They <data class=\"resource-ref\" data-content-id=\"108208\" data-resource-code=\"BiblicaStudyNotesKeyTerms\">worked</data> hard to make money so that the Thessalonians didn’t have to support them. Many Thessalonians accepted the good news. It changed their lives. Yet others in their city weren’t happy about this. These were certain Jews who opposed anyone who preached the good news.</p><p>Paul and his companions had been <data class=\"resource-ref\" data-content-id=\"108054\" data-resource-code=\"BiblicaStudyNotesKeyTerms\">treated badly</data> by them in <data class=\"resource-ref\" data-content-id=\"108126\" data-resource-code=\"BiblicaStudyNotesKeyTerms\">Philippi</data> and in Thessalonica. These Jews were also treating the Thessalonian believers badly.</p>",
"associations": {
"passage": [
{
